    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, I see where the confusion lies, and I apologize for the goblin distraction. Let's dive straight into the math, shall we? The formula for capacitive reactance, Xc = 1/(2πfC), is all about how the frequency of the alternating current (AC) affects the ability of the capacitor to resist that current.\n\nIn this formula, f represents the frequency of the AC, C is the capacitance, and Xc is the capacitive reactance. The frequency (f) is like the speed at which the AC is changing direction. Imagine the AC as a wave, and the frequency is how many waves pass a point in one second.\n\nNow, when the frequency increases, the wave passes the point more quickly, and the capacitor has less time to charge and discharge. It's like trying to catch a fast-moving goblin; the quicker it moves, the harder it is to catch it. In the context of the capacitor, a higher frequency means the capacitor can't charge up as much before the current changes direction again, making it easier for the AC to pass through.\n\nSo, in simpler terms, higher frequency means lower reactance because the capacitor can't resist the AC as much when the AC is changing direction more",
